"SuffixTree, Ukkonen's Suffix Tree Algorithm\n=============================================\n\n### Overview\n\nThe Ukkonen's suffix tree algorithm is a fast and efficient algorithm for constructing suffix trees from a given string. It is used to search for a pattern in a text by traversing the tree and checking if the pattern matches the text at each node.\n\n### Code\n\n```javascript\nconst SuffixTree = require('./SuffixTree');\n\nfunction buildSuffixTree(text) {\n    return new SuffixTree(text);\n}\n\nfunction searchSuffixTree(suffixTree, pattern) {\n    const index = 0;\n    const node = suffixTree.root;\n    while (index < pattern.length) {\n        if (node.start + index >= pattern.length || pattern[index] !== pattern[node.start + index]) {\n            break;\n        }\n\n        index++;\n        node = node.children[node.start + index];\n    }\n\n    if (index === pattern.length) {\n        console.log(`Found pattern at position ${index}`);\n    } else {\n        console.log(`Pattern not found`);\n    }\n}\n\n// Example usage\nconst text = 'banana';\nconst suffixTree = buildSuffixTree(text);\nsearchSuffixTree(suffixTree, 'ana');\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\nThe Ukkonen's suffix tree algorithm works by recursively building a tree where each node represents a suffix of the input string. The tree is constructed by iterating through each character of the input string and adding new nodes to the tree as necessary. The `Search` method then traverses the tree to find the node corresponding to the input pattern.\n\nThe algorithm uses a technique called \"suffix pointer\" to efficiently traverse the tree. The suffix pointer is a set of characters that are guaranteed to be present in the input string at each node. By using the suffix pointer, the algorithm can avoid unnecessary traversals of the tree.\n\nThe algorithm also uses a technique called \"canonization\" to simplify the tree and make it easier to traverse.\n\n### Active Point and Canonization\n\nThe active point is the node that corresponds to the current character being processed in the input string. The active point is used to determine whether a new node should be created or whether an existing node should be removed. The algorithm uses the canonization technique to simplify the tree and make it easier to traverse.\n\n### Why Implementations Need to Fix Bounding Variables\n\nImplementations may need to fix bounding variables to ensure that the algorithm works correctly. Bounding variables are used to determine the range of values that should be considered during the traversal of the tree. However, the bounding variables may need to be adjusted to account for any special cases or edge cases in the algorithm.\n\n### Update 2017-11-04\n\nA new use for suffix trees has been found, and the algorithm has been implemented in JavaScript. The JavaScript implementation includes debugging code to ensure that the algorithm works correctly. The implementation can be run with `node.js` to see the output."
